This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a normalized ground state solution for this equation. Notably, this work extends prior findings to accommodate exponential growth terms. Additionally, we establish the uniqueness of a normalized ground state solution when $\beta$ exceeds a certain threshold and demonstrate the continuity of a normalized ground state solution with respect to perturbation parameters. Lastly, we investigate an alternative equation and demonstrate the existence of a normalized ground state solution for that equation as well. A significant aspect of this research involves establishing link and variable changing techniques in a compact non-trivial domain, which are previously proven theorems. Hence, we can derive the existence of a normalized ground state solution for the original equation within a compact non-trivial domain. Moreover, we can determine the exact value of the generalized norm and establish the uniqueness of a normalized ground state solution when $\beta$ exceeds a certain threshold in both a compact non-trivial domain and in $\mathbb{R}^4$. Additionally, we investigate an alternative equation and demonstrate the existence of a normalized ground state solution for that equation within a compact non-trivial domain as well. Lastly, we investigate a further alternative equation and demonstrate the existence of a normalized ground state solution for that equation in $\mathbb{R}^4$. Notably, this work extends prior findings to accommodate exponential growth terms. Additionally, we establish the uniqueness of a normalized ground state solution for both a compact non-trivial domain and in $\mathbb{R}^4$ when $\beta$ exceeds a certain threshold. Moreover, we establish the continuity of a normalized ground state solution with respect to perturbation parameters

